        "weaviate_id": "46a81f05-72fe-48bd-b82d-44500365fd01",
        "paragraph_text": "Thin interbeds of pebble conglomerate and sandstone, occurring in the basal parts of the lower and upper Gowganda diamicts, occasionally exhibit cross bedding indicating a phase of turbulent currents at the time of deposition. Ripple cross lamination in the sandstone is more clearly visible in X ray radiograph Fig.4. At a few places near the top of the argillite unit thin interbeds of sandstone show ripple cross laminations of herringbone type. Among the secondary structure physical load casts PETTIJOHNand POTTER, 1963, are common and particularly occur in the interbedded argillite sandstone sequence in the upper part of the argillite unit Plate IC. Saucer shaped bodies occur as loadcasts in cross section in the central part of Plate ID, but isolated, resembling ball and pillow structure, in the layer immediately above as well as in the upper part. This may possibly represent a case where load casts, after having been dislocated subsequently, have given way to ball and pillow structure, a possibility indicated by POTTERand PETTIJOHN1963, p.140. Several other layers in the same outcrop, however, remain undisturbed. Ball and pillow structures occur at several places in the middle and upper parts of the argillite unit. The long axes of the saucer shaped bodies, usually parallel to bedding, are sometimes folded and the arrangement is chaotic. This structure has been attributed to sliding or slumping see POTTER and PETTIJOHN, 1963 or foundering of a sand layer due to shock KUENEN,1958. Complex folding of lamination observed in the laminated argillite in some places is attributed to \"soft sediment deformation\".",
        "text_type": "weaviate_text",
        "legend_id": null
      },
